00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:18.120
buildings and there are accounts out

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:20.280
there, and that might be the thing that

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:23.280
you need to help you get back on your

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:26.240
feet after a month or two. And my fourth

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.839
suggestion, if you could not work out

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:32.600
things with your credit card companies,

00:11:28.839 --> 00:11:34.960
look at your credit cards and see which

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:37.440
is your oldest credit cards and try to

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:41.000
maintain those. Remember, age of credit

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:43.440
is 15% of your credit score. So, your

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:46.320
oldest accounts, if you can hold on to

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.440
those and pay those what you can, that

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.200
might be your best option if you have to

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:53.320
let some go. Now, most of the times when

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.720
I look at people's credit report, their

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:57.880
oldest cards could be subprime cards,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:12:00.520
which could possibly be a good thing

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:03.640
because if you start missing payments on

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:05.320
credit card lines, if you have top-tier

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.640
credit card companies like Chase or

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.800
American Express, if they're seeing that

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:12.040
you missed a payment even with another

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:14.160
company, they can shut down your current

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:16.640
credit card lines. With those subprime

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:19.280
credit cards, they generally don't do

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:21.760
that. So, trying to save your oldest

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:25.480
credit card lines is something that you

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:27.320
might want to try to do. And if you have

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:30.040
some of those credit cards shut down on

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:31.680
you, like a Chase or American Express,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:34.600
before it shows up on your credit

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.480
report, which is like 30 days that that

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:40.080
account closed, see if you can hurry up

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:42.240
and replace that credit card line with

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:44.400
something else. Try to apply somewhere

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.320
else according to where your credit

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:50.960
score is at that time before all of that

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:52.920
show up. So, hopefully this information

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:54.520
was helpful. If you have any questions

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:56.440
or comments, leave it in the comment

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.080
section. For more detailed questions for

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:59.680
me, you can go to my website, and all

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.280
your questions go directly to my cell

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:04.880
phone. I hope to see you guys in the

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.880
next video. Stay tuned.

